 that  we get the equipment that we need but as  with everything when you have a new  virus which poses new challenges you  have to keep things under review  listening I just can't help feeling as  if it looks like we're always playing  catch-up we thought we had enough  protective equipment then we realized we  didn't we didn't think testing was a  priority now we do we're trying to scale  it up rapidly we're still reviewing the  evidence on face mask we're still  looking at international travel why is  it that we seem to have acted more  slowly than other countries well the  first thing to say is on personal  protective equipment we did have good  stocks and those stocks have built up  partly to deal with the pandemic flu  pandemic partly because of the  experience of preparing for a no  deal-breakers well that's what I'm  telling you we did have those stop  suddenly built them up it is the case  that with testing we've always  recognized its importance and we've been  making sure that we can develop five  pillars in order to ensure that we can  have a range of tests appropriate to the  circumstances and it is the case that  when it comes to areas like the the  wearing of masks we've been looking at  the scientific evidence but it comes  back to to our very first sort of stage  of this conversation when you were quite  rightly probing and asking when will  certain restrictions be lifted and I  said that we wanted to follow the advice  and to only proceed when the scientific  advice was clear there is a temptation  of course to act and to act in certain  circumstances because of you know  understandable pressure from  commentators and elsewhere but our  approach is to be guided by the science  and to move in a way which is not a  knee-jerk but considered but at the same  time it is the case that there is urgent  active and energetic work across the NHS  across the scientific community and  across government in order to ensure  that where their science is clear where  our action needs to be urgently we're  taking it which is why we have  to increase the number of ventilators in  the NHS and bring new ventilators online  it's why were increasing our testing  capacity and it's why also we are making  sure that as well as bringing PPE from  abroad we're also increasing our  capacity to manufacture it at home okay  now in the Sunday Times today they  claimed that Boris Johnson skipped five  meetings on coronavirus the meetings of  Cobra the emergency committee an unnamed  senior Downing Street adviser is quoted  in the newspaper saying there's no way  you're at war if your Prime Minister  isn't there and what you learn about  Boris walls he didn't share any meetings  he liked his country breaks he didn't  work weekends there was a real sense  that he didn't do urgent crisis planning  it was exactly like people feared he  would be now back in 2016 you withdrew  your support for Boris Johnson's  leadership bit saying he cannot provide  the leadership or build the team for the  task ahead given what you've seen in the  last few months were you right or wrong  about that totally wrong the the point I  mentioned earlier that they the Sunday  Times story was had one or two off beam  elements to it  this I think is the one that some  wildest off beem the idea that the Prime  Minister skipped meetings that were  vital to our response to the coronavirus  I think is grotesque the truth is that  the there are meetings across government  some which are chaired by the health  secretaries on which are chaired by  other ministers but the Prime Minister  took all the major decisions and I think  anyone who considered what happened to  the prime minister just a little while  ago nobody can say that the practice  throwing heart and soul into fighting  this virus his leadership has been clear  it's been inspirational at times and I  think that actually nothing is more off  beam than the suggestion the Prime  Minister was anything other than an  energetic determined focused and strong  in his leadership against this virus
 

